1	# Propose E2E cases for a pull request
2	
3	You propose end-to-end test cases for one Discourse Graphs pull request. A person approves or
4	rejects every case you propose before anything runs, so propose what a careful QA engineer would
5	want to check, and nothing to pad the list. You do not write steps: a later agent writes them by
6	driving the running app.
7	
8	The inputs below are the ticket's Done When bullets, the PR diff, the existing kit (its cases,
9	including ones a person rejected and why), and the app the kit targets.
10	
11	## What to produce
12	
13	Answer with one JSON object and nothing else, matching the schema at the end.
14	
15	### 1. Acceptance cases: one or more per Done When bullet
16	
17	- Copy each bullet's number and text exactly into `doneWhen`.
18	- Write one case per bullet. Split a bullet into several cases only when its parts need different
19	  starting states or different user journeys (for example add, edit, open and remove).
20	- If a bullet can't be checked through the app's UI (unit tests, code structure, docs), don't write
21	  a case for it. List it in `notE2E` with the reason.
22	
23	### 2. Surface areas: existing features the diff could break
24	
25	A surface area is a user-facing feature that existed before this PR and that the diff can reach.
26	For each one give:
27	- `area`: the feature as a user names it ("Create Relation dialog", not a file name).
28	- `why`: one sentence naming the exact mechanism the diff changed and how that could break the
29	  feature. Name the changed function or component and the existing caller or behaviour at risk.
30	- `files`: the changed files that tie it to the diff. Every path must appear in the diff.
31	- `probability` and `impact`, 1 to 3 each, as bare integers:
32	  - probability 1 = standard change, low uncertainty; 2 = edge cases or partial unknowns;
33	    3 = shared code, persistence, sync, settings, patches to a dependency, or high ambiguity.
34	  - impact 1 = cosmetic or easy workaround; 2 = partial feature loss or manual workaround;
35	    3 = blocks the feature, loses or corrupts data, or exposes something it shouldn't.
36	
37	Ground every area in the diff. Do not add plausible areas because they are common in other apps.
38	If you suspect a risk you can't tie to a changed line, put it in `assumptions` instead.
39	
40	### 3. Regression cases: only for areas scoring 3 or more (probability × impact)
41	
42	- One case per area. Put boundary and trigger variants of the same risk in that case's `variants`,
43	  not in extra cases.
44	- Use these techniques where they fit: equivalence classes and boundaries for inputs (empty, one,
45	  many, very long, unicode, duplicate names); state transitions for anything with a lifecycle
46	  (create, edit, rename, delete, undo, reload, close and reopen, sync); negative and error paths.
47	
48	### 4. The value bar: every case must answer all four
49	
50	- `protects`: the observable behaviour it protects.
51	- `failsWhen`: the credible regression that makes it fail.
52	- `whyNew`: why no existing kit case catches that. Name the closest existing case by id. If an
53	  existing case already asserts the same observable outcome, don't propose a case: list it in
54	  `extend` with that case's id and what to add to it.
55	- `level`: `e2e` only when the failure first shows in a user journey. If a unit or integration
56	  test would catch it first, don't propose it; say so in `notE2E`.
57	
58	### 5. Respect earlier decisions
59	
60	The existing kit lists rejected cases with reasons. Don't propose them again, or anything the
61	reason rules out. Two common reasons: "another case covers it" and "this PR doesn't reach it".
62	
63	### 6. Ask instead of guessing
64	
65	If you can't tell how a user reaches a feature, or what the expected result is, write the case
66	anyway and add a question to `questions` naming the case. The person answers before steps are
67	written.
68	
69	## Writing the cases
70	
71	- `title`: a sentence about behaviour a user sees ("A text shape offers Edit link, like a geo
72	  shape"), not an implementation detail.
73	- `given`: the starting state, assuming a fresh vault or graph with only the kit's fixtures.
74	- `intent`: what the user does, in their own words and terms (menus, commands, buttons), in a few
75	  short sentences. No selectors.
76	- `then`: what the user sees afterwards that proves it. Check the result where it lives (the note,
77	  the page, the canvas file), not only a toast.
78	- Each case must run on its own, in any order.
79	- `priority`: P0 data loss, security or no safe workaround; P1 core or frequent behaviour; P2
80	  secondary behaviour with a workaround; P3 rare or cosmetic. A missing workaround alone keeps a
81	  case at P1 or lower.
82	
83	## Schema
84	
85	```json
86	{
87	  "areas": [
88	    { "key": "short-kebab-key", "area": "", "why": "", "files": [""], "probability": 1, "impact": 1 }
89	  ],
90	  "cases": [
91	    {
92	      "kind": "acceptance | regression",
93	      "doneWhen": { "number": 1, "text": "" },
94	      "areaKey": "short-kebab-key",
95	      "title": "", "given": "", "intent": "", "then": "",
96	      "variants": [""],
97	      "priority": "P0 | P1 | P2 | P3",
98	      "protects": "", "failsWhen": "", "whyNew": ""
99	    }
100	  ],
101	  "extend": [{ "caseId": "", "add": "" }],
102	  "notE2E": [{ "doneWhen": 4, "reason": "" }],
103	  "questions": [{ "about": "case title or area key", "question": "" }],
104	  "assumptions": [""]
105	}
106	```
107	
108	`doneWhen` is required for acceptance cases and absent for regression cases. `areaKey` is required
109	for regression cases and optional for acceptance cases. `variants` may be empty.
110	
111	
112	## Inputs
113	
114	### App
115	obsidian
116	
117	### Done When
118	1. A text shape exposes the same **Edit link** action as a geo shape.
119	2. A user can add, edit, open, and remove a link on a text shape with the same behavior and visual affordance as a geo shape.
120	3. The link persists when the canvas is closed and reopened.
121	4. Unit tests cover link persistence and the text-shape eligibility behavior.
122	
123	### Ticket
124	# ENG-2228 Add link support to tldraw text shapes in Obsidian
125	
126	## Problem
127	
128	* Text shapes cannot link to a page or URL, so a simplified canvas narrative cannot open the detailed source represented by its text.
129	* Geo shapes already provide this interaction, but text shapes do not.
130	
131	## Solution
132	
133	* Add the existing geo-shape link action and behavior to Obsidian text shapes.
134	* Reuse the geo-shape link code path instead of creating a separate text-shape implementation.
135	
136	## Done When
137	
138	* A text shape exposes the same **Edit link** action as a geo shape.
139	* A user can add, edit, open, and remove a link on a text shape with the same behavior and visual affordance as a geo shape.
140	* The link persists when the canvas is closed and reopened.
141	* Unit tests cover link persistence and the text-shape eligibility behavior.
142	
143	## Out of Scope
144	
145	* Converting a text shape into a Discourse Graph node.
146	* Adding a page picker or a new link format.
147	* Adding links to other unsupported shape types.
148	
149	## Notes
150	
151	* Page links are the primary expected target, but this ticket retains the existing geo-shape URL workflow.
152	
153	
154	### Existing kit
155	No kit yet.
